          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em net
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+set"} directed set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequences"} sequences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = \mathbb{N}_{\leq}
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            for formalization of the concept of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} convergence
          %p
            Nets are also called
            %em Moore–Smith sequences
            and are equivalent (in a certain sense) to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+filters"} proper filters
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Filter"}
            below), their
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eventuality+filters"} eventuality filters
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EventualityFilter"}
            below).
          %p
            The concept of nets is motivated from the fact that where plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequences"} sequences
            detect
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
            properties in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact+metric+spaces+are+equivalently+compact+metric+spaces"} sequentially compact metric spaces are equivalently compact metric spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact+space"} sequentially compact
            neither implies nor is implied by being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact+space"} sequentially compact space
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "sequentially+compact+topological+space#Examples"} Examples and counter-examples
          %p
            Inspection of these counter-examples reveals that the problem is that sequences indexed by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            may be “too short” in that they cannot go deep enough into uncountable territory, and they are “too slim” in that they proceed to their potential limiting point only from one direction, instead of from many at once. The use of general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+sets"} directed sets
            for nets in place of just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            for sequences fixes these two issues.
          %p And indeed, as opposed to sequences, nets do detect
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the topology on general topological spaces (prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologyDetectedByNets"}
                below),
            %li
              %p
                the continuity of functions between them (prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContinuousFunctionsDetectedByNets"}
                below),
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+space"} Hausdorff
                property (prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NetsDetectHausdorff"}
                below),
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compactness
                (prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompactSpacesEquivalentlyHaveConvergetSubnets"}
                below).
          %p
            While the concept of nets is similar to that of sequences, one gets a cleaner theory still by considering not the nets themselves but their “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filters"} filters
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            which they eventually meet” (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EventuallyAndFrequently"}
            below), called their
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eventuality+filters"} eventuality filters
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EventualityFilter"}
            below). For example equivalent filters are equal (in contrast to nets) and (unless in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small
            (not a proper class).
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#DirectedSets Directed sets
          #DirectedSet.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+set"}> directed set
                )
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+set"} directed set
              is
            %ul
              %li
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preordered+set"} preordered set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi D
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ≤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (D, \leq)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                equipped with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"} reflexive
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive"} transitive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            %p such that
            %ul
              %li
                every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
                has an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/upper+bound"} upper bound
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in D
                there exists
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in D
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq c
                and
                = succeed "." do
